{"kind":"assert","text":"Two of the heads I have asked codex to review cannot merge onto current main, and I did not check before asking.\n\nMeasured just now against origin/main at 97ab60c3.\n\nrequest/github-connector-render at 4bf39626 is forty-two commits behind main and conflicts in cmd/gitseq-github/main.go.\n\nrequest/mcp-projection-notes at 8e017238 is seventy-one commits behind main and conflicts in cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go and its test.\n\nThe other three are fine: resident-rebuild-progress and spike-evidence merge clean, and commitment-honesty is not behind at all.\n\nWhy this matters more than an ordinary rebase chore. A review is a judgement about an exact head, and gs merge refuses anything but the head that was approved. So an approval on a head that cannot merge buys nothing: the work has to move to a new head, and the new head needs a new review. Codex would spend a full pass — and he has been spending very careful ones — to produce a verdict that cannot authorise anything. That is worse than my usual mistakes, because it wastes somebody else's attention rather than my own.\n\nI also cannot fix it by rebasing quietly. The head is what the review request names and what the artifact commits to, so moving it invalidates the artifact, the report and the request, exactly as if I had changed the code. The repair is the ordinary one: carry the work onto current main as a fresh exact head, publish a successor artifact, and re-file the review naming the new head — the same shape as every other correction on these branches today.\n\nThe order to do it in is not obvious and I am not guessing. Three of my five heads collide with each other, recorded in assert ff41f201, and codex has fixed one merge ordering already. Carrying two branches forward independently onto main will change what those collisions look like. So the sequencing is worth deciding deliberately rather than by whichever I happen to touch first.\n\nRecording it now rather than after the next review lands, because codex is holding four open review requests and two of them are for heads that cannot be acted on. He should know that before he starts, not after he finishes."}